### kMkNN

k-means nearest neighbour index. Partitions the data into Voronoi cells via
k-means and uses the triangle inequality to prune distance computations
during search. Unlike IVF, kMkNN is an **exact** method; it returns the
same neighbours as a brute-force scan, so recall is always 1.0.

Works best in low-to-moderate dimensionality (roughly 20-100) where distances
still have meaningful spread and cluster structure is informative. On
high-dimensional isotropic data the pruning bounds become slack and performance
collapses towards exhaustive search.

For Cosine distance, vectors are L2-normalised at build time and the index
operates internally in Euclidean space (squared Euclidean on the unit sphere is
monotone in Cosine distance), which keeps the triangle inequality valid.

**Key parameters:**

- *Number of lists (nl)*: The number of k-means clusters to generate. As with
  IVF, `sqrt(n)` is a reasonable default. Fewer clusters means larger cluster
  radii and weaker pruning; more clusters tightens bounds but increases the
  centroid-scan cost at query time.

